Whatever you're dealing with anything with any autoimmune issues though, and you could read this in in the book you have to address the gut.

00:00:13,630 --> 00:00:22,630
So anybody have food allergies here? Food allergies have to do. I don't believe I come from a very Christian perspective.

00:00:22,630 --> 00:00:30,630
I don't believe that God made us to have food allergies. So part of the problem is that our food has been changed, right?

00:00:30,630 --> 00:00:46,630
Gluten per se is the same gluten that we were exposed to even a hundred years ago, and the amount of gluten that we're exposed to today has changed because of white flour and such.

00:00:46,630 --> 00:00:53,630
So I can understand that entire perspective, but you have to deal with gut damage.

00:00:53,630 --> 00:01:08,630
So your gut is a barrier, just like your skin is a barrier. So if you walked around and kept cutting your skin, cutting your skin every day and you just cut your skin in different places, you're going to end up with an infection sooner or later.

00:01:08,630 --> 00:01:18,630
Well that's what happens in your gut. Your gut is a barrier, your skin is a barrier, your brain has a barrier from the blood for different chemicals to get through.

00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:29,630
When you break those barriers, we end up having problems. So if your gut, your intestinal villi, your cells are very close together, normally food.

00:01:29,630 --> 00:01:42,630
So let's use gluten as an example. Gluten is a protein. So the protein of gluten, think of that as a great big log chain.

00:01:42,630 --> 00:01:52,630
And that's the protein that you chew up and you swallow. Now it goes into your body and your stomach, and that's where you talk about pH.

00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:58,630
Your stomach has to have a very low pH. It's the only part of your body that you need to have a pH of about 1.0.

00:01:58,630 --> 00:02:06,630
And that's where digestion starts to take place. Well it actually starts to take place in your mouth, but it starts to break down those proteins.

00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:18,630
It breaks down the chain into individual links. So you want the chain into individual links. On a protein each link is going to be the acid.

00:02:18,630 --> 00:02:26,630
And those amino acids are chained together in a certain format that make it what it is as a protein.

00:02:26,630 --> 00:02:35,630
So as you break it down and you chew it up and you swallow it and your hydrochloric acid, your stomach breaks it down and your digestive enzymes further break it down.

00:02:35,630 --> 00:02:43,630
It gets down into individual links, or maybe at most two links bound together, that's called a dipeptide.

00:02:43,630 --> 00:02:53,630
That's the only way it should get across your gut border. So food, when you eat it, it's broken down into small particles.

00:02:53,630 --> 00:03:00,630
Easiest way to understand. And then it gets brought across the gut wall in the smallest particles.

00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:11,630
But when I have a damaged gut wall, so instead of my attestinal villi cells being very close together, they're damaged and they're separated. Some cells are dead and they're separated.

00:03:11,630 --> 00:03:20,630
Now, things are coming across the blood, the gut barrier, much larger than they ever were supposed to.

00:03:20,630 --> 00:03:28,630
So, when that chain is at 15 links and it was not supposed to ever cross the gut barrier, it can fit right through.

00:03:28,630 --> 00:03:39,630
And then your immune system recognizes it as an enemy, like what the heck is that? Let's kill it. That's what your immune system does, right?

00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:47,630
So it fires an immune reaction against that long chain peptide and you develop a food sensitivity.

00:03:47,630 --> 00:03:58,630
Oh, I'm sensitive to gluten. Every time I eat gluten, I have a reaction. And that could be a sliding scale of sensitivity, depending on how damaged my gut barrier is.

